---
import OCR from '../../layouts/OCR.astro';
import snapshot from '../../../public/ocr-eval/results.json';
import examples from '../../../public/ocr-eval/examples/index.json';
const sections = [['tasks', 'Tasks'], ['sampling', 'Sample selection'], ['metrics', 'Error rates'], ['ranking', 'Ranking'], ['inference', 'Model settings'], ['reproduce', 'Reproducibility']];
---
<OCR title="Methodology" description="How ScandiOCR Eval selects documents, runs OCR models, computes CER and WER, and ranks results." active="methodology">
  <header class="page-heading"><div><h1>Methodology</h1><p>From a document image to a comparable score.</p></div></header>
  <div class="documentation-layout">
    <nav class="contents" aria-label="On this page"><h2>On this page</h2>{sections.map(([id, name]) => <a href={`#${id}`}>{name}</a>)}</nav>
    <article class="prose">
      <section id="tasks"><h2>Benchmarks and tasks</h2>
        <p>A <strong>benchmark</strong> is a fixed collection of OCR tasks. A <strong>task</strong> pairs a dataset with the image input given to the model. The <a href="/ocr-eval/benchmarks/scandinavian/">Scandinavian OCR v1 benchmark</a> contains 11 tasks from 9 datasets. Danish, Norwegian and Swedish benchmarks contain their respective subsets. Each benchmark page lists its exact tasks and links to examples and source data.</p>
        <p><strong>Cropped text line.</strong> The model receives an image of one line and returns that line’s text. Existing line images are used where available; page datasets are cropped using their annotations.</p>
        <p><strong>Whole document page.</strong> The model receives an entire page and returns its text in reading order. The score measures text accuracy, including omissions, additions and ordering errors. It does not separately evaluate bounding boxes, tables or layout structure.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="sampling"><h2>Sample selection</h2>
        <p>Each dataset has a fixed evaluation sample shared by all models. The targets are <strong>1,000 lines</strong> or <strong>100 pages</strong> per dataset, using seed <code>{snapshot.run.seed}</code>. Modern Danish has 977 available lines, so all are used.</p>
        <p>Line-only datasets use uniform sampling across the source split, except Riksarkivet, where sampling is balanced across 19 source files. Page samples are balanced across documents. Historical Danish lines are sampled from the 100 selected pages; modern Danish lines are drawn from the full collection.</p>
        <p>The <a href="/ocr-eval/datasets/">dataset catalogue</a> lists each source split and reference type. A source labelled <code>train</code> is used for evaluation here; it is not an official held-out test split. The benchmark does not train or fine-tune models on these samples.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="metrics"><h2>Character and word error rates</h2>
        <p><strong>CER</strong> measures edits at the character level. <strong>WER</strong> measures edits at the word level. Both use the minimum number of substitutions, deletions and insertions needed to turn a prediction into its reference.</p>
        <div class="metric-equation"><span>Error rate</span><span>=</span><span class="fraction"><span>substitutions + deletions + insertions</span><span>reference characters or words</span></span><span>× 100%</span></div>
        <p>Counts are accumulated across samples within each dataset before division: these are <strong>corpus error rates</strong>, not averages of per-image percentages. A CER of 5% means five character edits per 100 reference characters. Lower is better; insertions can push a score above 100%.</p>
        <h3>Text normalization</h3><p>Prediction and reference are Unicode NFC-normalized, and consecutive whitespace is collapsed to one space. Leading and trailing whitespace is removed. Case, punctuation, diacritics and historical spelling are preserved. Metrics are computed with <code>jiwer</code>.</p>
        <h3>Unsuccessful predictions</h3><p>A failed or timed-out attempt is scored as an empty prediction against its full reference. It is not dropped from the score or retried. Repetition and other incorrect output remain part of the evaluation.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="ranking"><h2>Aggregation and ranking</h2>
        <p>The table shows the selected error rate for every task alongside an overall score. Task cells always contain corpus CER or WER in percent, regardless of aggregation. Click a dataset name to read its description, the arrow beneath it to sort by that task, or a score to inspect example predictions. Bold marks the lowest error rate on a task; darker red shading indicates a worse position among the models evaluated on that task. Shading is relative within each column, not a shared error-rate scale.</p>
        <h3>Mean</h3><p>The overall score is the arithmetic mean of task error rates, with each task weighted equally. Task scores of 2% and 8% produce a mean of 5%, regardless of image counts. A dataset evaluated as both lines and pages contributes two tasks. Lower is better.</p>
        <h3>Borda rank</h3><p>Models are ranked by error rate on each task, then these positions are averaged. A score of 1 means first on every task. Exact ties share the average of their occupied positions: two models tied for first each receive 1.5. Lower is better.</p>
        <h3>Win rate</h3><p>Each task supplies a pairwise comparison between models: lower error wins, and a tie awards half a win to each. A Bradley–Terry model is fitted to these comparisons using 200 minorization–maximization iterations. For model strengths sᵢ and sⱼ, the fitted probability of i beating j is sᵢ / (sᵢ + sⱼ). The displayed score averages this probability over all other eligible models and expresses it as a percentage. Higher is better. This is a fitted win probability, not the fraction of tasks won outright; no confidence intervals are estimated.</p>
        <p>The aggregation choices follow the <a href="https://huggingface.co/spaces/RyeAI/danish-asr-leaderboard">Danish ASR leaderboard</a>. Borda ties use average positions here, and a numerical floor of 10⁻¹² keeps Bradley–Terry strengths positive. Win rate is unavailable if fewer than two models qualify.</p>
        <h3>Incomplete evaluations and ties</h3><p>All three overall scores require a completed, valid evaluation on every task in the selected benchmark. Only these fully evaluated models form the Borda and win-rate comparison pool. Models with partial results remain visible with their available task scores, but receive no overall score. A dash means no score is available; it never means zero error. Per-image failures inside a completed evaluation are already counted in its task error rate as described above.</p>
        <p>Sorting uses full precision before display rounding. Equal scores sort by model name. Search only hides rows: it does not change aggregate scores, task shading, or the comparison pool. Borda and win-rate scores can change when models enter or leave the benchmark, even if their transcription results are unchanged. The comparison page retains separate mean scores for lines and pages.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="inference"><h2>Model settings</h2>
        <p>Results cover OCR engines, specialised recognition models and vision-language models. Each uses its adapter’s preprocessing, prompt and decoding settings. Image resolution and output-token limits can differ between models; these are comparisons of the recorded configurations.</p>
        <p>The local sweeps used an NVIDIA RTX 3090 with 24 GB of VRAM and 32 GB of system RAM. Memory and time limits kept inference bounded. Exact settings, model revisions, batch sizes and runtime limits are retained per evaluation in the <a href="/ocr-eval/results.json">results download</a>.</p>
        <p>Hosted evaluations use the Mistral OCR 4.1 API and GPT-6.1 Sol through the standard OpenAI API with low reasoning. Both receive the original image dimensions. Mistral returns raw Markdown; GPT-6.1 Sol uses transcription prompts, with output limits of 8,192 tokens for cropped lines and 16,384 for pages, including reasoning tokens.</p>
        <p>Dataset descriptions explain relevant reference and training context, including the NorHand specialist model, line cropping, and references extracted from PDFs. No uncertainty intervals or statistical-significance claims are reported for these fixed samples.</p>
      </section>
      <section id="reproduce"><h2>Reproducibility</h2>
        <p>The published scores combine {snapshot.source_runs.length} runs on the same fixed samples. Each exported result was checked against its report and sample manifest before publication.</p>
        <ul class="resource-list"><li><a href="https://github.com/V4ldeSalnikov/OCR-eval">Evaluation code ↗</a><span>Dataset loaders, model adapters and scoring implementation.</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/V4ldeSalnikov/OCR-eval/tree/main/results">Hosted evaluation reports ↗</a><span>Full predictions, references, scores and settings for the hosted models.</span></li><li><a href="/ocr-eval/results.json" download>Results JSON ↓</a><span>Scores, sample counts, source revisions, manifest hashes, inference settings and run provenance.</span></li><li><a href="/ocr-eval/datasets/">Dataset sources →</a><span>Original collections, transcriptions and the subsets used here.</span></li></ul>
        <p>The <a href="/ocr-eval/compare/">comparison view</a> includes three real examples per task and dataset: {examples.counts.examples} images and {examples.counts.predictions} saved predictions. Cases are selected by a fixed hash of their identifiers, independently of model scores. These examples illustrate model behaviour; the leaderboard still scores the complete frozen samples.</p>
        <p class="small-copy">The results snapshot contains aggregate scores and provenance. The separate example gallery republishes selected benchmark images, references and predictions with <a href="/ocr-eval/examples/ATTRIBUTION.md">source attribution</a>. Highlighting compares words after Unicode and whitespace normalization; switching it off shows the saved prediction text. Per-example CER and WER are the original report metrics. Adapter changes after a run do not change its saved scores or recorded source hashes.</p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</OCR>
